Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to use the **`mapTransitions`** helper to tag the first four items of an array with the CSS‑transition category `"transition"`.

```js
// 1️⃣  Your source array – replace this with whatever list you actually have.
const sourceArray = [
  'header',
  'nav',
  'main',
  'footer',
  'sidebar',
  // …more items if needed
];

// 2️⃣  Call the helper.  
//     - `category` is the value you want every key to map to.
//     - `limit` is the number of elements to include (as a string, per the API).
const transitionMap = mapTransitions({
  category: 'transition',
  limit: '4',          // <-- first 4 elements only
});

// 3️⃣  Inspect the result
console.log(transitionMap);
/*
  Expected output:
  {
    header: 'transition',
    nav:    'transition',
    main:   'transition',
    footer: 'transition'
  }
*/
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1️⃣  | You supply the array that contains the items you want to map. | The helper will iterate over this array. |
| 2️⃣  | `mapTransitions` is invoked with the desired category (`"transition"`) and the limit (`"4"`). | The helper builds an object where each key is one of the first four array elements and each value is `"transition"`. |
| 3️⃣  | The resulting object is logged. | You can now use this mapping in your CSS‑transition logic (e.g., to look up which elements should animate). |

> **Tip:** If you need to change the number of elements or the category, just adjust the `limit` or `category` values in the call. The helper will automatically rebuild the mapping for you.

